WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:10.069
Alexander the Great was one of the most extraordinary conquerors in world history, capturing a vast area from Greece to India in a little over ten years.

2
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:21.582
What is even more amazing is that Alexander did all this as a young man: he was only 20 years old when he took control of the Greek army and just 32 when he died in Persia.

3
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:33.211
Between 340 and 323 BC, Alexander united the Greek states and spread Greek culture and ideas to foreign lands such as Egypt, Syria, and Persia.

4
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:45.972
Although nobody was able to command his empire after his death—it eventually dissolved—Alexander's conquests were so successful and happened so quickly that he was celebrated as a hero for centuries.

5
00:00:45.973 --> 00:00:53.256
Like his idol the legendary Greek Achilles, Alexander is remembered as a nearly invincible warrior.

6
00:00:53.257 --> 00:00:56.582
As a boy, Alexander learned the Greek myths.

7
00:00:56.583 --> 00:01:06.142
He learned about Zeus, the supreme god of the Greeks, who struck people with lightning if they displeased him, and about Athena, the goddess of wisdom.

8
00:01:06.143 --> 00:01:15.740
His favorite hero, however, was Achilles, a fearless warrior who fought in the Trojan War and was immortalized in the famous poem The Iliad.

9
00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:27.348
Alexander's mother told him he was descended from Achilles, and his father, King Philip of Macedonia, said his family was descended from a long line of warrior ancestors.

10
00:01:27.349 --> 00:01:38.105
With tales of Greek gods ever present in his mind and a powerful ruler as his father, Alexander truly believed the gods had destined him for greatness.

11
00:01:38.106 --> 00:01:44.612
King Philip had successfully expanded the borders of Macedonia and captured many Greek cities and colonies.

12
00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:50.761
As a teenager, Alexander learned military strategy from his father and then helped him plan battles.

13
00:01:50.762 --> 00:02:01.256
Alexander also spent three years studying with Aristotle, the great scientist and philosopher of the day, and grew to love natural science and Greek culture and art.

14
00:02:01.257 --> 00:02:08.414
At age 16, he joined his father's army and commanded the cavalry that defeated the Greeks in the Battle of Chaeronea.

15
00:02:08.415 --> 00:02:20.126
After this success, his father planned to move east and conquer Persia, but before he could do so, he was—shockingly—murdered by one of his own bodyguards.

16
00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:31.733
Immediately, Alexander was named king of Macedonia, but many Greek cities, sensing an opportunity for revenge, started to rebel against Macedonian rule and tried to fight back.

17
00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:35.460
However, Alexander was shrewd.

18
00:02:35.461 --> 00:02:43.007
He had learned from his father that the smartest way to silence rebels was to quickly make a show of force and subdue them.

19
00:02:43.008 --> 00:02:50.770
So he led his army into the rebellious city of Thebes and demolished the city, burning much of it to the ground.

20
00:02:50.771 --> 00:02:54.214
Alexander then continued his journey, marching into Asia.

21
00:02:54.215 --> 00:03:00.828
Crossing through a province of the Persian Empire, Alexander came upon the former palace of an ancient king.

22
00:03:00.829 --> 00:03:05.373
Outside the palace was an ox-cart tied to a pole with a thick knot.

23
00:03:05.374 --> 00:03:11.983
According to legend, whoever could untie the knot—called the Gordian knot—would rule Asia.

24
00:03:11.984 --> 00:03:19.633
Rather than spend a lot of time trying to untie the knot, Alexander simply cut it with one slash of his sword.

25
00:03:19.634 --> 00:03:28.459
When he heard thunder and lightning hours later, he said it was proof that the gods were pleased with his dramatic solution to the difficult problem.

26
00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:33.011
Believing that the gods were on his side, Alexander's confidence soared.

27
00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:40.074
He rampaged through one territory after another, putting his warrior skills to practical use.

28
00:03:40.075 --> 00:03:52.327
Deciding he wanted to conquer the prosperous island city of Tyre (off the coast of present-day Lebanon), he ordered his men to build long land bridges from the coast to the shores of Tyre.

29
00:03:52.328 --> 00:03:59.716
Once they reached the city's outer walls, Alexander's army used battering rams and catapults to seize the city.

30
00:03:59.717 --> 00:04:08.821
After his victory at Tyre, he drove his men farther south, directly into Egypt, where he went to visit an Egyptian oracle in an oasis.

31
00:04:08.822 --> 00:04:20.920
No one knows what the oracle said to him, because Alexander kept it a secret, but from that time on, people were convinced the oracle had proclaimed him descended from the gods.

32
00:04:20.921 --> 00:04:31.955
For the rest of the journey he decorated his helmet with ram horns as proof that he was descended from Zeus as well as from Amun, the supreme god of the Egyptians.

33
00:04:31.956 --> 00:04:40.076
Alexander also founded his own city, Alexandria, on the northern edge of Egypt, which later became a central trading city.

34
00:04:40.077 --> 00:04:51.232
Immediately moving on, Alexander and his troops traveled east and eventually penetrated the farthest boundaries of Persia—what is now Afghanistan and western India.

35
00:04:51.233 --> 00:05:03.990
In the most significant military battle of the entire journey, Alexander defeated Darius III and the Persian army at the Battle of Gaugamela in an area now known as northern Iraq.

36
00:05:03.991 --> 00:05:11.251
The Persians had a massive army, with chariots, 40,000 cavalry, and elephants carrying weaponry.

37
00:05:11.252 --> 00:05:20.587
Alexander's army was much smaller, but they were superiorly trained and advanced swiftly, each man carrying only a short spear and shield.

38
00:05:20.588 --> 00:05:32.131
As the Persians waited in the middle of a large plain, Alexander's army descended from the hills in two fast columns, running straight into the center of the Persian army.

39
00:05:32.132 --> 00:05:38.479
Surprised by the speed of the Greeks, the Persians panicked and fled the battlefield.

40
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:52.951
Now, Alexander had most of Asia secured, and he took loads of silver, gold, and other treasures from the ancient city of Persepolis in Persia and continued eastward, finally ending his journey in India.

41
00:05:52.952 --> 00:06:04.386
With his great kingdom established—stretching all the way from Greece to India—Alexander began attempts to blend together the many different cultures within his empire.

42
00:06:04.387 --> 00:06:09.004
He encouraged trade and cooperation between the Greeks and the Persians.

43
00:06:09.005 --> 00:06:15.495
He married a princess from a newly conquered region and included many non-Greeks in his own army.

44
00:06:15.496 --> 00:06:24.392
He also spread the ideas of Aristotle and other Greek philosophers, artists, and scientists to the people of every region he conquered.

45
00:06:24.393 --> 00:06:36.983
Alexander returned to the Persian city of Babylon with his wife, but he fell sick with fever and chills and died shortly afterward at the young age of 32—his death is still a mystery.

46
00:06:36.984 --> 00:06:46.749
His short yet vibrant life had come to an abrupt end, and he left behind an expansive but extremely unstable empire with no successor to manage it.

47
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:52.804
His top generals became leaders in different regions of the empire, but they fought among themselves.

48
00:06:52.805 --> 00:06:56.626
By 300 BC, the empire had dissolved.

49
00:06:56.627 --> 00:07:09.018
Nevertheless, Alexander had successfully carried Greek customs and thought eastward and had set the stage for the next phase of world history, the Hellenistic Age, which lasted three hundred years.

50
00:07:09.019 --> 00:07:19.433
During this period, Greek culture—including art, religion, and philosophy—combined with the existing cultures of conquered lands to create a rich, new culture.

51
00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:25.783
The scientific achievements of the Greeks, most notably of Aristotle, were especially influential in Persia.

52
00:07:25.784 --> 00:07:34.808
Thanks to Alexander's efforts to unite Greek and Persian cultures, the Hellenistic Age saw advances in science that might not have been achieved otherwise.

53
00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:48.631
Euclid developed the principles of geometry, Archimedes discovered many laws of physics, and most importantly, Aristarchus suggested (for the first time in history) that all planets revolve around the sun.

54
00:07:48.632 --> 00:08:01.948
Alexander earned the name "Alexander the Great" because of his skill and intelligence on the battlefield and because his conquests, still arguably the greatest in world history, changed the course of human civilization.

55
00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:12.730
Although he was only one man and ruled for only 12 years, the story of Alexander the Great will no doubt continue to be told for many generations.

